#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""sinko — parental controls on top of Pi-hole v6.

Commands
  setup        Create/refresh the Pi-hole groups, lists and rules this add-on uses (and run 'pihole -g': one run at a
               time, so it waits up to 30 minutes for a run that is already going).
  configure    Apply the Pi-hole settings the add-on needs (web root, local name).
  run          Background scheduler (timers, bedtime, updates, requests from the page). Run by systemd.
  status       Print the current state.
  doctor       Check the installation and print anything that needs fixing.
  diagnose     Read-only report on why a service is still reachable after it was blocked.
  watch        Follow the DNS log to see whether a device's queries reach the box.
  update       Update to the newest release (--check, --yes, --ref REF, --force); rolls back by itself if it fails.
               --ref pins the box to that release or branch once the update has worked (a failed update leaves the
               choice as it was); --ref latest follows the newest release again. Needs more than 200 MB free.
  rollback     Go back to the previous version stored on this box (also needs more than 200 MB free).
  repair       Install this version again from the copy on the box when the page and the program are of different
               versions (an update was cut short). Nothing is downloaded. The scheduler runs it by itself after ten
               minutes of such a state, at most once an hour.
  selfcheck    Check that the installation works (run after every update): Pi-hole objects, the scheduler (up for a
               while and finishing its passes), every file of the page.
  telemetry    The optional anonymous counter: on, off (and the counter forgets this box), status, payload (what
               would be sent), reset-id (the counter forgets the old id, a new one is made).
  learn        Learning new addresses of blocked apps with the small AI model on the box (off until switched on): on,
               off, status, list (what was learned), forget NAME (allow a learned address again; it is not learned
               again). Nothing is sent anywhere.
  box-info     Print what the page may know about this box (/pb/box.json); --write puts it in the page folder.
               The scheduler refreshes that file every five minutes: the page reads it as the scheduler's heartbeat.
  use-mac      Re-register children that were added by IP address under their MAC address.
  remove       Delete everything this add-on created inside Pi-hole. A device that also has a group of yours stays
               (without this add-on's groups); one that was registered only for this add-on goes.

How it works
  Every service (YouTube, TikTok, ...) has its own Pi-hole group "pb-svc-<id>"
  that owns one blocklist. The lists stay enabled at all times so gravity always
  contains their domains; a service is blocked by ENABLING its group and allowed
  by disabling it. Kid devices are members of every pb-* group. Toggling a group
  takes effect immediately (FTL reloads on every API write) — no DNS restart.

  Shared state (timer, bedtime schedule, update status, the parent's requests) is
  stored as JSON in the description of the disabled group "pb-state", so the web
  page and this daemon share it through the Pi-hole API without any extra port or
  database. The page only asks (a request marker); the daemon acts once and clears
  the marker. Runtime data of the daemon itself lives in /var/lib/sinko (root only).

  For the page's card "What Sinko did for you" the daemon also keeps a ledger of counts
  (stops by kind and app, time the children's internet was off, bedtime nights kept)
  in /var/lib/sinko/savings.json, and copies it into the disabled group "pb-ledger",
  which only a signed-in parent can read.

Only the Python 3 standard library is used.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

